---
import { expand } from "../lib/glossary";

interface Props {
  name: string;
  /** Marks the two or three that actually carried the work in this role. */
  starred?: boolean;
}

const { name, starred = false } = Astro.props;
const full = expand(name);
---

{
  /*
    Terms with an expansion render as <abbr>, which is the semantic element for
    this and gives screen readers the full phrase for free. The dotted underline
    is there so a reader knows there is something to hover, rather than having
    to discover it by accident. Where hovering is not possible the underline
    comes off, for the reason in the style block.
  */
}
<li class={starred ? "tag tag--starred" : "tag"}>
  {
    full ? (
      <abbr class="tag__abbr" title={full}>
        {name}
      </abbr>
    ) : (
      name
    )
  }
</li>

<style>
  .tag__abbr {
    text-decoration: underline dotted var(--amber-dim);
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
    cursor: help;
  }

  .tag--starred .tag__abbr {
    text-decoration-color: var(--amber-hi);
  }

  /*
    A title attribute has no tap, long press or focus behaviour on iOS or
    iPadOS. On a device that cannot hover, the dotted underline and the help
    cursor therefore advertise something the reader has no way to open, on 12
    chips on /projects and 7 on the home page. Take the advertisement away
    rather than leave a dead affordance: a fake button that does nothing is
    worse than an honest ornament, and a fake tooltip is the same trade. The
    <abbr> and its title both stay, because that pair is what a screen reader
    reads and it costs a touch reader nothing.

    /skills answers this same query by rendering the expansion as real text.
    That was tried here too, and measured, and it is wrong for these chips.
    They are a scan row: one line of seven at 375px, white-space: nowrap, and
    the point of them is being read in three seconds. Expanding them turned two
    or three chips per card into bordered paragraphs, took the tallest tech row
    on /projects from 77px to 228px, took a 23px row to 191px, and grew the
    page from 10165px to 10741px at 375px. Nothing overflowed, so the numbers
    are not the whole objection: a definition in a bordered box sitting next to
    a plain chip reads as the more important of the two, which is backwards.
    The stacked entries on /skills take the same sentence as one more dim line,
    so that is the page where the expansions get told.

    The query is hover rather than the pointer: coarse used elsewhere in this
    repo, because hovering is the capability the tooltip depends on and pointer
    size is not. Phones and tablets report both, so on the devices this is
    actually for the two queries pick out the same machines.
  */
  @media (hover: none) {
    .tag__abbr {
      text-decoration: none;
      cursor: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
